# Captured from vanilla 26.2 (protocol 776) survival oracle.
# Packet: clientbound container_set_slot, from `item replace … with`
# minecraft:diamond_pickaxe[minecraft:tool={rules:[
# {blocks:"#minecraft:incorrect_for_diamond_tool",correct_for_drops:false},
# {blocks:["minecraft:stone","minecraft:obsidian"],speed:12.5f}
# ],default_mining_speed:1.5f,damage_per_block:3}]
#
# ..        VarInt window id  (session-scoped; not compared)
# ..        VarInt state id   (container revision; not compared)
# ..        i16    slot index (not compared)
# 01        VarInt stack count = 1
# c6 07     VarInt item registry id 966 = minecraft:diamond_pickaxe
# 01        DataComponentPatch: 1 added
# 00        DataComponentPatch: 0 removed
# 1c        component type id 28 = minecraft:tool
#
# then Tool.STREAM_CODEC:
# 02                    VarInt rule count = 2
# rule 1:
# 00                  HolderSet discriminator 0 = named tag follows
# .. <utf8>           Identifier "minecraft:incorrect_for_diamond_tool"
# 00                  optional(f32) speed: absent
# 01 00               optional(bool) correct_for_drops: present, false
# rule 2:
# 03                  HolderSet discriminator = 2 direct holders + 1
# 01                  Holder = block registry id 1, AS-IS (minecraft:stone)
# c1 01               Holder = block registry id 193, AS-IS (minecraft:obsidian)
# 01 41 48 00 00      optional(f32) speed: present, 12.5
# 00                  optional(bool) correct_for_drops: absent
# 3f c0 00 00           f32 default_mining_speed = 1.5
# 03                    VarInt damage_per_block = 3
# 01                    bool can_destroy_blocks_in_creative = true
#
# Note the two HolderSet shapes and the two independently-absent
# optionals: rule 1 denies drops with no speed, rule 2 sets a speed with
# no verdict. Collapsing either pair is the bug this fixture catches.
# Note above all that the direct holders are NOT offset by one: only the
# set-size discriminator is. `holderSet` delegates to
# `ByteBufCodecs.holderRegistry`, which writes the raw registry id, not to
# `ByteBufCodecs.holder`, which reserves 0 for an inline definition and so
# writes id + 1. We shipped `id + 1` first; the hermetic test agreed with
# it because it encoded the same way, and these captured bytes are what
# disproved it (`01`, not `02`, for stone).
# Note also that can_destroy_blocks_in_creative is present on the wire
# even though the SNBT never mentioned it — it is a codec field with a
# default, not an optional.
00 02 00 24 01 c6 07 01 00 1c 02 00 24 6d 69 6e
65 63 72 61 66 74 3a 69 6e 63 6f 72 72 65 63 74
5f 66 6f 72 5f 64 69 61 6d 6f 6e 64 5f 74 6f 6f
6c 00 01 00 03 01 c1 01 01 41 48 00 00 00 3f c0
00 00 03 01
